In most cases, a call to <code>ToString()</code> on an array indicates a developer is interested in returning the contents of the array as a String. However, a direct call to <code>ToString()</code> on an array will return a string value containing the array's type.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following code will output <code>System.String[]</code>.
<pre>
String[] stringArray = { "element 1", "element 2", "element 3", "element 4" };
System.Diagnostics.Debug.WriteLine(stringArray.ToString());
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
.NET has several different solutions to converting object arrays to their string equivalent. The generic approach to converting an array of objects to its String equivalent is to iterate through the array to get the value of each element. Each element is then appended to a StringBuilder or String object. This example converts a String[] Object to a comma-delimited String:

<b>Example 2 (Generic Approach):</b>
<pre>
	String[] stringArray = { "element 1", "element 2", "element 3", "element 4" };

    StringBuilder builder = new StringBuilder();
    for (int index = 0; index &lt; stringArray.Length; index++)
    {
        builder.Append(stringArray[index]);
        if (index != stringArray.Length - 1)
            builder.Append(", ");
    }

    String stringRepresentation = builder.ToString();
</pre>

The <code>stringRepresentation</code> object contains the value:
<pre>
element 1, element 2, element 3, element 4
</pre>

<b>Example 3 (String[] Specific):</b>
Alternatively, .NET provides the String.Join function to achieve the same result for String[] objects:

<pre>
	String[] stringArray = { "element 1", "element 2", "element 3", "element 4" };
	String stringRepresentation = String.Join(", ", stringArray);
</pre>

The <code>stringRepresentation</code> object contains the value:
<pre>
element 1, element 2, element 3, element 4</pre>

The root cause of a Castle ActiveRecord injection vulnerability is the ability of an attacker to change context in the Castle ActiveRecord query, causing a value that the programmer intended to be interpreted as data to be interpreted as a command instead. When a Castle ActiveRecord query is constructed, the programmer knows what should be interpreted as part of the command and what should be interpreted as data. Castle ActiveRecord statements can enforce this behavior by disallowing data-directed context changes and preventing nearly all Castle ActiveRecord injection attacks. Castle ActiveRecord treats command values separate from the user data to be operated upon. These Castle ActiveRecord statements are translated into traditional parameterized Castle ActiveRecord code upon execution.

The previous example can be rewritten using a normal Castle ActiveRecord statement (instead of concatenating user supplied strings) as follows:

<pre>
...
string userName = ctx.getAuthenticatedUserName();
string queryString = "SELECT * FROM items WHERE owner = :owner" +
				" AND itemname = :itemname";

SimpleQuery&lt;Item&gt; queryObject = new SimpleQuery(queryString);
queryObject.SetParameter("owner", username);
queryObject.SetParameter("itemname", Itemname.Text);

Item[] items = (Item[])queryObject.Execute();

...
</pre>

More complicated scenarios, often found in report generation code, require that user input affect the command structure of the Castle ActiveRecord statement, such as the addition of dynamic constraints in the <code>WHERE</code> clause. Do not use this requirement to justify concatenating user input into query strings. Prevent Castle ActiveRecord injection attacks where user input must affect statement command structure with a level of indirection: create a set of legitimate strings that correspond to different elements you might include in a Castle ActiveRecord statement. When constructing a statement, use input from the user to select from this set of application-controlled values.

The root cause of a SubSonic injection vulnerability is the ability of an attacker to change context in the SubSonic query, causing a value that the programmer intended to be interpreted as data to be interpreted as a command instead. When a SubSonic query is constructed, the programmer knows what should be interpreted as part of the command and what should be interpreted as data. SubSonic statements can enforce this behavior by disallowing data-directed context changes and preventing nearly all SubSonic injection attacks. SubSonic treats command values separate from the user data to be operated upon. These SubSonic statements are translated into traditional parameterized SubSonic code upon execution.

The previous example can be rewritten using a normal SubSonic statement (instead of concatenating user supplied strings) as follows:

<pre>
...
string userName = ctx.getAuthenticatedUserName();
string query = "SELECT * FROM items WHERE owner = @owner" +
				" AND itemname = @itemname";

Object[] parameters = { username, Itemname.Text };

IDataReader responseReader = new InlineQuery().ExecuteReader(query, parameters);
...
</pre>

More complicated scenarios, often found in report generation code, require that user input affect the command structure of the SubSonic statement, such as the addition of dynamic constraints in the <code>WHERE</code> clause. Do not use this requirement to justify concatenating user input into query strings. Prevent SubSonic injection attacks where user input must affect statement command structure with a level of indirection: create a set of legitimate strings that correspond to different elements you might include in a SubSonic statement. When constructing a statement, use input from the user to select from this set of application-controlled values.

Description.Explanation:
The use of certain encoding functions will prevent some, but not all cross-site scripting attacks. Depending on the context in which the data appear, characters beyond the basic &lt;, &gt;, &amp;, and " that are HTML-encoded and those beyond &lt;, &gt;, &amp;, ", and ' that are XML-encoded may take on meta-meaning. Relying on such encoding functions is equivalent to using a weak deny list to prevent cross-site scripting and might allow an attacker to inject malicious code that will be executed in the browser. Because accurately identifying the context in which the data appear statically is not always possible, the Fortify Secure Coding Rulepacks report cross-site scripting findings even when encoding is applied and presents them as Cross-Site Scripting: Poor Validation issues.

Cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when:

1. Data enters a web application through an untrusted source. In the case of reflected XSS, the untrusted source is typically a web request, while in the case of persisted (also known as stored) XSS it is typically a database or other back-end data store.

<Paragraph>
In this case, the data enters at <Replace key="SourceFunction" link="SourceLocation"/> in <Replace key="SourceLocation.file"/> on line <Replace key="SourceLocation.line"/>.
</Paragraph>
2. The data is included in dynamic content that is sent to a web user without validation.
<Paragraph>
In this case, the data is sent at <Replace key="SinkFunction" link="SinkLocation"/> in <Replace key="SinkLocation.file"/> on line <Replace key="SinkLocation.line"/>.
</Paragraph>
The malicious content sent to the web browser often takes the form of a JavaScript segment, but can also include HTML, Flash or any other type of code that the browser executes. The variety of attacks based on XSS is almost limitless, but they commonly include transmitting private data such as cookies or other session information to the attacker, redirecting the victim to web content controlled by the attacker, or performing other malicious operations on the user's machine under the guise of the vulnerable site.

